you probably remember back to when EA  and Bioware were first marketing Mass  Effect Andromeda and telling us all the  things we were in store for in the new  game there was one word that Bioware  devs kept using during that time that  made me start to worry they go  everything was going to be bigger  at one point Bioware sad that one of  them multiple explorable story worlds  would be the size of Dragon Age  Inquisition as a whole as someone who  poured 120 hours into Inquisition and  enjoyed it very much that should have  excited me but it just set off alarm  bells in my head and made me wonder what  exactly is in all this extra space it  turns out the answer that question is  not heaps if you want to get an idea of  what I mean you can subscribe to us on  YouTube and check out the Mass Effect  Andromeda content we have there but for  now let me tell you where I'm going with  all this I can't help but feel like Mass  Effect Andromeda is unfinished the  sci-fi RPG elements are still there but  it feels like many of the things that  made the series special have been gutted  the engaging dialogue from colorful  exciting and sometimes tragic characters  is rare at best you can no longer kid  out your companions and they're leveling  options are severely limited which made  me feel even more apathetic toward them  and the things Bioware has expanded on  like crafting and large-scale world  exploration feel tedious it's such a big  step back not just for Mass Effect about  for Bioware and while playing Andromeda  I can't help but wonder how this  happened  personally I blame that word bigger now  I understand that when a lot of you guys  hear bigger you hear more bang for your  buck and I get that if you're paying for  a full pressed game 60 hours of gameplay  sounds better than 20 but personally  I'll always take quality over quantity  if a game is already great at 10 hours I  wouldn't want a developer to potentially  make it mediocre by drawing it out to 20  for the sake of more playtime for  example if games like Journey Firewatch  or portal would Rona unnecessarily it  may not have had the same impact  Andromeda includes huge sections of  chasing objective markers and talking to  uninteresting NPCs it means there's a  lot of extra hours of game in there sure  but it feels like unsatisfying padding  that doesn't contribute much to the  tenants of  make mass effect games so good meeting  fascinating people and feeling like a  hero I don't know about you but I don't  feel particularly heroic trying to make  my way to get another Waypoint to  deliver random assorted supplies while  switching the Nomad between regular mode  and lumbering slight hill climbing mode  for 10 minutes apiece of course these  sorts of missions are optional but in  Andromeda you can never tell if a  missions going to be worthwhile until  you're actually doing it sometimes side  quests that seem trivial at first end up  revealing really cool content but even  more often side missions with  interesting story justifications still  devolve into boring fetch quest gameplay  once you get started I felt the same way  about nerman sky when that nade release  I mean that whole shtick was you'd have  18 quintillion planets to explore which  is great but it led me to thinking all  right what's on them size and scope mean  nothing if you don't have a compelling  reason for said size big for the sake of  big is well exactly the problem with  Andromeda that's not to say big can't be  done well games like The Witcher 3 and  the Skyrim are great examples of worlds  that feel full and vibrant while being  pretty damn massive I loved seeing the  terrain change under my feet while  traversing Tamriel and in both games you  often stumble upon intriguing characters  and side quests that make it well worth  exploring the sprawling game world  the problem with bigger isn't just empty  spaces in between points of interest  either it's also the features that get  sidelined during development in the  pursuit of making everything else more  expansive Mass Effect Andromeda  animation issues are a perfect example  if we take the word of ex Bioware  animator Jonathan Cooper  it sounds like Bioware had likely  intended to code every facial animation  individually but with 1200 plus speaking  characters this year size of that task  meant it didn't happen if Cooper's right  the bulk of the work was left up to  automated algorithms and the end result  is well this fortunately a little better  now that there's been a patch but you  can still see the effects back in  November of last year ea said it would  happily delay Mass Effect Andromeda for  a second  if it felt the game needed more time to  be improved it was originally delayed  from 2016 until the first quarter of  this year and as you probably noticed it  did indeed come out after just one delay  so EA must have decided they were happy  enough with the final product to not  push the release date back again I don't  think any alma de is a bad game there  are some great character moments says  that sense of wonder when you're landing  on a new planet and I personally really  enjoyed establishing settlements  it just feels hollar some missions wrap  up with an unceremonious email in your  inbox rooms are mostly filled with  characters who don't speak and while you  can now ask the twelve hundred odd  speaking characters more questions than  you could in any previous Bioware game I  found myself unwilling to broach further  conversation because the NPC's weren't  interesting enough that I wanted to know  more about them I mean I lost anyway  because I'm a completionist but honestly  I don't really care about Joe schmo's  reasons for coming to Andromeda when I  know I'll never speak to him again I'm  sure I'll keep playing Andromeda in the  hopes that it improves and because of my  love for the original Mass Effect  trilogy  but it is hard not to think about what  the game could have been the combat  system improvements demonstrate that the  current Bioware team can deliver  something better for the mass effect  formula but I wish it had gone further  than just improved combat to be fair a  few of my friends have told me that Mass  Effect Andromeda totally kicks into gear  at the forty fifty hour mark so make it  that what you will  oh that's just my opinion though so let  me know where you land on Andromeda and  if there are any games that you think do  big really well or not so well I'll  catch you again next time
 

